.TH FKSLURP 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkslurp, fkslurpfile \- read a whole file into a new fkstring
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkslurp(int " fd );
.BI "fkstring *fkslurpfile(const char *" path );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkslurp ()
reads everything from the file descriptor
.IR fd ,
from its current offset to end of file, and returns it as a new
.BR fkstring .
Embedded NUL bytes are preserved.
Reads interrupted by a signal
.RB ( EINTR )
are retried.
.PP
.BR fkslurpfile ()
opens
.I path
for reading, reads it with
.BR fkslurp (),
and closes it again.
.PP
For a regular file, the size reported by
.BR fstat (2),
less the current offset, sets the size of the first buffer, so the file
is normally read into a single allocation.
The size is only a hint: reading always continues until
.BR read (2)
reports end of file, so a file that grows while it is being read, or a
.I /proc
file that reports a size of 0, is still read in full.
Pipes, sockets, and terminals give no hint; for them, reading starts with
a 4096-byte buffer
.RI ( _slurptry )
that grows as described in
.BR fkstring (3).
.PP
If the data fills much less of the buffer than was allocated, the excess
is released, by the same rule as
.BR fkstrtrunc (3).
.SH RETURN VALUE
A new
.BR fkstring ,
which is empty if there was nothing to read.
.PP
On error, these functions return NULL with
.I errno
set, and any bytes already read are discarded.
.BR fkslurpfile ()
also returns NULL if
.I path
is NULL, without changing
.IR errno .
.SH ERRORS
.BR fkslurp ()
can fail with any error of
.BR read (2),
including
.B EAGAIN
on a nonblocking descriptor with no data ready.
.BR fkslurpfile ()
can also fail with any error of
.BR open (2).
.PP
Neither returns on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH NOTES
.BR fkslurpfile ()
opens
.I path
with
.BR O_CLOEXEC .
.SH BUGS
Both functions read until end of file, and some files never end.
Do not use them on
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
a pipe from
.BR yes (1),
or anything else that never reports end of file: the buffer grows until
memory runs out, and the process is terminated by the library or, sooner,
by the system.
Use
.BR fkstrread (3)
with an explicit byte count, or
.BR fkcat (3),
instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *conf = fkslurpfile("/etc/hostname");
\&
if (conf == NULL)
    perror("/etc/hostname");
else
    fkstrwrite(STDOUT_FILENO, conf);
fkstrdestroy(conf);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR read (2),
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstrread (3),
.BR fkreadline (3),
.BR fkcat (3)
